Falsifies `byte_sink_contract::verification::an_end_of_stream_decodes_exactly_from_its_one_message`.

The defect: the decoder reads the opcode alone and ignores the rest of an end-of-stream message.
A writer can then put a status in the two reserved words, or a length in the first word, and
every sink takes it as an ordinary end.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert_eq!`, on any end-of-stream first word paired with a
non-zero second or third word.

diff --git a/crates/byte_sink_contract/src/lib.rs b/crates/byte_sink_contract/src/lib.rs
--- a/crates/byte_sink_contract/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/byte_sink_contract/src/lib.rs
@@ -259,7 +259,7 @@ pub fn unpack(w0: u64, w1: u64, w2: u64, out: &mut [u8; INLINE_MAXIMUM]) -> Msg
             out[8..].copy_from_slice(&w2.to_le_bytes());
             Msg::Bytes(n)
         }
-        OPERATION_EOF if w0 == eof() && w1 == 0 && w2 == 0 => Msg::Eof,
+        OPERATION_EOF => Msg::Eof,
         _ => Msg::Malformed,
     }
 }
